WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.272
We just mentioned that switches have speed capabilities of 10 Mbps to 10 Gbps. Of course, this depends on the type of switch you get. Most switches have multiple capabilities.

00:12.272 --> 00:23.199
Take for example, a typical switch. It may have a total of 24 or 48 ports, and most of these ports will operate at Fast Ethernet: 100 Mbps.

00:23.199 --> 00:26.889
A few ports can operate at Gigabit Ethernet speed.

00:26.889 --> 00:40.536
This allows an administrator to use 100 Mbps ports for devices with 100 Mbps network cards, and, for the faster devices, Gigabit Ethernet ports can be used.

00:40.536 --> 00:48.880
One thing to look out for in these switches is the LED lights. In most cases, a green light indicates a connection at full speed.

00:48.880 --> 00:58.394
A yellow light indicates a connection at less than full speed. For example, if you are seeing yellow on a Gigabit Ethernet connection,

00:58.394 --> 01:07.114
chances are the network cable plugged into the port or the network card from the originating device is only capable of Fast Ethernet speeds.

01:07.114 --> 01:20.471
A major principle to remember about any network connection is this: With any connection, the speed will only be as good as the slowest device in the connection.

